{"authored" [{"question" "q-332944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/state.json" "file_sha256" "9dace835f01e493c85b5129767d17e3f39193e5304d4a1c4d47150ef65882de0" "basis" 1683 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2(2x+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}(2x+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}(1+{t}^{3}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/state.json" "file_sha256" "9dace835f01e493c85b5129767d17e3f39193e5304d4a1c4d47150ef65882de0" "basis" 1683 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "212b7e4f79df61a66e280d6bf20727c8bc84b7688c1e27463efaab86fd828d3c" "basis" 1683 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332944" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/state.json" "file_sha256" "9dace835f01e493c85b5129767d17e3f39193e5304d4a1c4d47150ef65882de0" "basis" 1683 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332944" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6t^2(1+t^3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/state.json" "file_sha256" "9dace835f01e493c85b5129767d17e3f39193e5304d4a1c4d47150ef65882de0" "basis" 1683 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\displaystyle J(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x,$ calculate $J'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$ by differentiating under the integral sign." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/example-21649.json" "file_sha256" "11544e15bff8d0ae5600dc5ab4aaca020736ed7ac9f42d586054f8f206290268" "basis" 1683 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute $J'(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nJ'(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Bigl[(\\cos t+2x)^2\\Bigr]\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)^{2}] & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x) \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1}+{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nJ'(t) =-2\\sin t\\,(\\cos t+1).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/example-21649.json" "file_sha256" "11544e15bff8d0ae5600dc5ab4aaca020736ed7ac9f42d586054f8f206290268" "basis" 1683 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R$\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)$\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/example-21732.json" "file_sha256" "f927da4dda4a629a9253de4e6b862729d1494e9033fa93c0279db6db8a8dcd0c" "basis" 1683 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\nf_1(x,t) = t\\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in \\mathbb R$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_1$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f_1}{\\partial t} = \\cos x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0.$ And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_1(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\nf_2(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq (0,1),$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_2$ is undefined when $x = t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, $f_2$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\nf_3(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq [0,1],$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_3$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f_3}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{2(x - t)}{(1 + (x - t)^2)^2}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0.$ And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_3(t)$ for all $t \\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/example-21732.json" "file_sha256" "f927da4dda4a629a9253de4e6b862729d1494e9033fa93c0279db6db8a8dcd0c" "basis" 1683 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042732/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-332944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-332944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2(2x+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}(2x+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}(1+{t}^{3}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-332944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06c605f197e6afc58e929997062db95782ec1df80319e33f560544c9b4cb2e52"} {"question" "q-332944" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-332944" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6t^2(1+t^3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}] & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{t}\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06c605f197e6afc58e929997062db95782ec1df80319e33f560544c9b4cb2e52"} {"question" "q-334955" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334955" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1+e^t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}] & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{t}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06c605f197e6afc58e929997062db95782ec1df80319e33f560544c9b4cb2e52"} {"question" "q-334972" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334972" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2e^t(1+e^t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{1}^{2}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}+{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =6{t}^{2}(\\frac{7}{3}+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}(3{t}^{3}+7).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06c605f197e6afc58e929997062db95782ec1df80319e33f560544c9b4cb2e52"} {"question" "q-334935" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334935" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14t^2+6t^5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}] & =2(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =4t(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}4t(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4t{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-1}^{0}+{t}^{2}x{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =4t({t}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =t(4{t}^{2}-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06c605f197e6afc58e929997062db95782ec1df80319e33f560544c9b4cb2e52"} {"question" "q-334939" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334939" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4t^3-3t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 1$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=x{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂[1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "06c605f197e6afc58e929997062db95782ec1df80319e33f560544c9b4cb2e52"} {"question" "q-334667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-334667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046669/state.json" "file_sha256" "09af0b2ad7de13398107c832cac31381e9f7de59ba6133ee92d7acd96af64b18"} {"question" "q-335253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{